# Build-context exclusions for every image built from this repository
# (infra/docker/api, infra/docker/auth, infra/docker/php-ci — all use the
# repository root as their context).
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS NOT MERELY AN OPTIMISATION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without it, whatever happens to be sitting in the working tree is copied into
# the image. That is a correctness and a security problem, not just a slow
# build:
#
#   * bootstrap/cache/packages.php is Laravel's package manifest. Run
#     `composer install` once WITH dev dependencies -- which CI does, and which
#     anyone debugging locally does -- and it lists dev-only providers. Copy
#     that file into a --no-dev image and `package:discover` aborts the build
#     with "Class Laravel\Pail\PailServiceProvider not found". Encountered
#     exactly that way, and the message points at the package rather than at
#     the stale file that caused it.
#
#   * .env is the local development environment file, complete with APP_KEY
#     and whatever credentials are in it. Copying it into an image bakes those
#     secrets into a layer, where they survive being overwritten later and can
#     be read by anyone who can pull the image.
#
#   * vendor/ and node_modules/ from the host may have been built for a
#     different PHP or Node version, or with different flags, and would
#     silently overwrite what the build just installed.

# Version control and tooling
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.editorconfig
.devcontainer
.github
.idea
.vscode

# Dependencies -- every image installs its own
**/vendor
**/node_modules

# Build output and framework caches. bootstrap/cache is the dangerous one; see
# above. The entrypoint rebuilds all of these at container start anyway.
**/bootstrap/cache/*
**/.next
**/dist
**/build
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.phpunit.cache
**/.phpunit.result.cache

# Runtime state. storage/ is a mounted volume at run time, so anything here
# would be shadowed immediately -- but logs and uploads have no business in an
# image regardless.
**/storage/logs
**/storage/framework
**/storage/app

# Secrets. .env.example and .env.production.example are templates with no real
# values and are deliberately NOT excluded -- the image documentation refers to
# them.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
!**/.env.production.example
**/auth.json

# Documentation and knowledge artifacts -- large, and no image needs them.
vault
graphify-out
source-docs
runbooks
docs
**/*.md
!apps/*/README.md

# Not built into any image
apps/mobile
tests
infra/k8s
infra/observability
